---
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../data/site';
---

<LegalPage
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description="The NeuroGIDAS website does not use cookies or tracking. Here is what that means for you."
  updated="October 20, 2026"
  summary={[
    'This website does not set any cookies.',
    'It does not use local storage, tracking pixels, or analytics.',
    'That is why there is no cookie banner.',
    'Sites we link to, like Instagram or GroupMe, may use their own cookies after you leave our site.',
  ]}
>
  <h2>What cookies are</h2>
  <p>
    Cookies are small files a website can save in your browser. Sites often use them to remember logins, save settings, or track
    what you do for advertising and analytics.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies on this site</h2>
  <p>
    We don’t use any. This site does not set cookies, and it doesn’t use similar tools like local storage, session storage,
    tracking pixels, or fingerprinting. We also don’t load any outside scripts, ads, or embedded social media posts that could
    set cookies for other companies.
  </p>
  <p>
    The only small scripts on the site run inside your own browser to do things like open the menu on phones, show which meeting
    is next, and let you tap through the schedule. They don’t save anything about you.
  </p>

  <h2>Why there’s no cookie banner</h2>
  <p>
    Cookie banners ask for your permission to store cookies. Since this site doesn’t store any, there is nothing to ask permission
    for.
  </p>

  <h2>Other websites</h2>
  <p>
    When you click a link to another site, such as GroupMe, Instagram, Google Calendar, or a research database, that site may use
    its own cookies. Their cookie and privacy policies apply there. You can control or delete cookies from other sites in your
    browser settings.
  </p>

  <h2>If this changes</h2>
  <p>
    If we ever add something that uses cookies, like analytics, we will update this page before we do it, change the date at the
    top, and ask for your consent where the law requires it.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions? Email <a href={`mailto:${LINKS.email}`}>{LINKS.email}</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
